      "model": "gpt-6.1-sol",
      "question": "For our transformer fleet planning, take the known failure modes for a transformer and tell me which of them we can actually watch on Transformer 1 at site MAIN with the channels it reports today. For each failure mode, list the two or three most diagnostic sensors among hydrogen, methane, ethane, ethylene, acetylene, co, co2, oxygen, nitrogen, dbds, dielectric_rigidity, interfacial_v, oti, oti_a, oti_t, ati, il1, il2, il3 and inut. Then mark each mode as 'well covered', 'partially covered' or 'blind', based on whether those sensors have data in the available window. Only list sensors that genuinely discriminate that mode, not ones that just correlate generally. If a mode's key sensor exists but has no values in the window, count the mode as blind rather than covered, and suggest which offline test or extra sample would close the gap.",
      "answer": "**Transformer 1, MAIN:** the available window is **15–19 January 2024**, with five daily records—not current live telemetry. All 21 channels you listed have **5/5 numeric values**; none is empty.\n\nThe stored catalog contains nine modes and is not exhaustive. “Well covered” below means diagnostic screening is supported, not that the fault is confirmed or precisely localized.\n\n| Known failure mode | Most diagnostic channels from your list | Coverage | Diagnostic limitation / gap-closing test |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Partial Discharge in Oil | **hydrogen, methane, acetylene** | well covered | Hydrogen-dominant gas patterns, with methane and acetylene distinguishing thermal/discharge alternatives. Electrical or acoustic PD testing confirms activity. |\n| Thermal Fault — Oil Overheating | **methane, ethane, ethylene** | well covered | Relative gas production discriminates lower- versus higher-temperature thermal faults. Repeat laboratory DGA establishes generation rates. |\n| Thermal Fault — Core/Winding | **ethylene, methane, co** | partially covered | Ethylene/methane indicate thermal activity; CO supports associated paper heating. They cannot locate the fault in core versus winding. Add excitation-current/core-ground checks and winding-resistance tests. |\n| Low-Energy Arcing (Sparking) | **acetylene, hydrogen, ethylene** | well covered | Joint gas proportions distinguish discharge severity better than acetylene alone. Confirm with repeat DGA and discharge investigation. |\n| High-Energy Arcing | **acetylene, ethylene, hydrogen** | well covered | The same gases discriminate high versus low energy through their relative proportions and trends—not separate single-gas alarms. Confirm with DGA and electrical investigation. |\n| Tap Changer Mechanical Wear | **None genuinely discriminating** | blind | Main-tank gases and phase currents do not diagnose mechanical wear. Perform tap-changer inspection, operating-timing and dynamic-resistance tests; separately sample its oil compartment if applicable. |\n| Oil Contamination / Moisture Ingress | **dielectric_rigidity, interfacial_v** | partially covered | Breakdown strength and interfacial tension screen contamination consequences but cannot identify moisture specifically. Obtain Karl Fischer water and particle-count tests. |\n| Cellulose Insulation Ageing | **co, co2** | partially covered | Carbon oxides and their relationship support paper-degradation screening, but are not specific enough to quantify ageing. Sample oil for furans, especially 2-FAL; measure paper degree of polymerization when accessible. |\n| Oil Dielectric Degradation | **dielectric_rigidity, interfacial_v** | well covered | Breakdown strength measures dielectric performance; interfacial tension supports oxidation/polar-contaminant assessment. Laboratory breakdown, acidity and dissipation-factor tests provide confirmation. |\n\n**No missing-value blindness was found.** Mechanical wear is blind because suitable diagnostic channels are absent, not because existing channels lack readings. Where only two—or no—channels genuinely discriminate a mode, I have not padded the list. DBDS identifies corrosive-sulfur risk, not generic dielectric degradation; temperature flags, ambient temperature and load currents mostly provide context rather than discriminate these cataloged modes.",
      "score": {
